import json
from pathlib import Path

# Topic-specific instructor drafts based on the assigned Notion sequence.
aero=[
('Paper aircraft: test one change at a time','Explain how lift, weight, thrust, and drag affect a paper aircraft.','Paper, tape, ruler, marked flight area','Build a baseline paper airplane. Record three flights from the same launch point. Change one wing or trim feature, repeat three flights, and compare average distance and flight stability.','The student identifies the changed variable, presents both sets of measurements, and supports a conclusion with evidence.','Sketch an improvement and predict its effect before the next test.'),
('Airframe assembly and balance','Assemble a straight airframe and explain how mass placement affects balance.','Approved airframe kit, ruler, square, balance stand','Dry-fit the fuselage and wings. Check alignment before fastening. Locate the center of gravity using the kit guidance. Move a test mass and record how the balance changes.','The student shows secure joints, symmetric alignment, and a measured balance location. The instructor approves the airframe before powered work.','Draw the airframe and label heavy components and the balance point.'),
('Electronics I: receiver and motor systems','Trace the power and signal paths of a receiver and motor system.','Approved receiver, transmitter, motor, controller, protected supply','With power disconnected and propellers removed, identify connectors and polarity. Follow the kit wiring diagram, request a wiring inspection, bind the receiver, and test the motor under direct supervision.','The student explains the wiring, demonstrates safe shutdown, and identifies the response to signal loss without a propeller installed.','Draw a labeled power and signal diagram.'),
('Electronics II: servos and control surfaces','Set up control surfaces with correct direction, neutral position, and travel.','Airframe, servos, linkages, transmitter, approved supply','Secure the airframe with the propulsion system disabled. Center servos, connect linkages, and test each control direction. Adjust endpoints to prevent binding and inspect the full travel.','The student identifies each control axis and demonstrates correct movement without binding. The instructor verifies the setup.','Explain the effect of reversing one control surface.'),
('Flight testing, inspection and repair','Use a supervised test plan to inspect, fly, evaluate, and repair an aircraft.','Approved aircraft, preflight checklist, marked operating area, repair materials','Write one test objective. Complete the instructor-led preflight and range briefing. Conduct an approved test, log observations, inspect the aircraft, and propose a repair or adjustment before the next flight.','The student follows the checklist, reports defects promptly, and distinguishes observations from explanations.','Write a short test report with the next change to investigate.'),
('Design an airframe in Onshape','Turn aerodynamic goals into a dimensioned CAD concept.','Onshape, reference dimensions, paper, calipers','Compare two wing or fuselage concepts against a stated mission. Sketch the chosen concept, build its geometry in a Part Studio, and use named dimensions for the critical features.','The student explains the mission tradeoff, shows an editable model, and identifies features that affect balance or manufacture.','Capture a labeled model view and explain one design tradeoff.'),
('Compare aerodynamic designs','Use a controlled comparison to evaluate two aerodynamic shapes.','Two comparable models, instructor-selected airflow demonstration or analysis tool','Choose one variable such as wing geometry. Keep the test conditions consistent and document the tool, units, and assumptions. Compare results and identify the limits of the demonstration. CAD modeling alone is not an aerodynamic simulation.','The student describes the controlled variable, explains the observed trend, and names one limitation of the method.','Recommend a design change and identify the evidence that supports it.'),
('Manufacture and assemble the prototype','Prepare a model for printing and assemble a testable prototype.','Onshape export, Bambu Studio, approved printer/material, assembly tools','Export parts in millimeters, inspect the slice, and obtain instructor approval. Use a prepared print if production exceeds class time. Inspect fit, assemble components, and record any manufacturing changes.','The student verifies scale, orientation, layer preview, and fit before requesting a flight-readiness review.','Update the assembly notes with the actual settings and fit results.'),
('Iterate from flight-test evidence','Improve an aircraft through measured, traceable revisions.','Prototype, test log, approved operating area','Review the previous test record. Select one improvement, save the original CAD version, change the design, and repeat the approved test. Compare the result with the baseline.','The student links a design revision to evidence and explains whether the result supports the original prediction.','Prepare a before-and-after comparison for the final presentation.'),
('Final aircraft challenge','Present a documented aircraft and demonstrate its readiness within instructor-approved limits.','Aircraft, portfolio, checklist, approved test area','Present the mission, design decisions, manufacturing notes, and test history. Complete the instructor-led readiness review, demonstrate the agreed challenge, and explain the next development step.','The student supports design decisions with evidence and meets the agreed challenge criteria while following operating procedures. This is an academy assessment, not a pilot certification.','Complete a portfolio summary with model links and test evidence.')]
flight=[
('The forces of flight','Explain the four forces and relate them to a simulator demonstration.','Paper models, whiteboard, simulator','Compare straight-and-level flight, climb, and descent. Predict how a change in pitch or power will affect the flight, then test the prediction in the simulator.','The student identifies the forces and explains the observed change without treating pitch and power as interchangeable.','Draw force diagrams for two flight conditions.'),
('Read the flight instruments','Recognize the main flight instruments and cross-check their indications.','Simulator cockpit or instrument images','Locate attitude, airspeed, altitude, heading, and vertical-speed indications. Compare readings in level flight, a climb, and a turn. Introduce a misleading indication and practice cross-checking.','The student reads each instrument correctly and explains why one indication should not be interpreted alone.','Label an instrument panel and write what each indication measures.'),
('Control the aircraft','Coordinate control inputs to establish and recover a desired attitude.','Simulator, control diagram','Identify pitch, roll, and yaw controls. Practice small inputs, level-off, coordinated turns, and returning to a stable attitude with instructor guidance.','The student names the control axis, makes measured corrections, and returns to the requested attitude.','Explain why large repeated corrections can make control less stable.'),
('Aircraft systems','Explain how the major aircraft systems support a flight.','Aircraft diagrams, simulator checklist','Trace fuel, electrical, propulsion, and flight-control systems on a diagram. Use the simulated aircraft checklist to connect a cockpit control to the relevant system. Discuss an instructor-selected failure scenario.','The student explains a system path and uses the appropriate checklist rather than improvising a procedure.','Make a one-page diagram of one system.'),
('Airports and surface operations','Interpret airport markings and plan a safe simulated taxi route.','Current airport diagram, simulator','Identify runways, taxiways, signs, and holding positions. Plan a route on the diagram, read back a sample clearance, and taxi it in the simulator while checking position.','The student identifies the route and required stopping points and asks for clarification when the instruction is unclear.','Annotate an airport diagram with a proposed taxi route.'),
('Understand airspace','Use a current chart to identify airspace and locate the applicable operating guidance.','Current FAA chart and instructor reference','Compare examples of controlled and uncontrolled airspace. Locate boundaries and altitude labels. Work through two instructor-selected route examples using the current FAA references.','The student reads the chart labels correctly and identifies the applicable reference rather than guessing an entry requirement.','Find and describe two charted airspace examples.'),
('Clear radio communication','Deliver concise simulated calls and accurate readbacks.','Headsets, sample airport scenarios','Practice who you are calling, who you are, where you are, and what you request. Role-play ground and tower instructions. Include an unclear message that requires clarification.','The student uses an organized call, reads back the critical instruction, and avoids guessing when uncertain.','Write and rehearse three scenario calls.'),
('Plan and follow a route','Use landmarks, headings, distance, and time to navigate a simulated route.','Current chart, ruler, simulator','Select a short route with visible checkpoints. Estimate heading, distance, and elapsed time. Fly the route, compare actual and estimated progress, and explain corrections.','The student tracks position using more than one cue and documents differences from the plan.','Prepare a navigation log for a new route.'),
('Weather and flight decisions','Interpret basic weather information and identify conditions requiring instructor review.','Current aviation weather examples, simulator','Read an instructor-selected observation and forecast. Identify wind, visibility, clouds, and significant changes. Compare two simulated scenarios and discuss how the conditions affect the plan.','The student explains the weather evidence and identifies uncertainty or a reason to change the plan.','Summarize a weather example and list questions for the instructor.'),
('Aircraft performance','Explain how weight, temperature, altitude, wind, and power affect aircraft performance.','Simulator, aircraft-specific performance examples','Compare two simulated takeoffs or climbs while changing one variable. Record the conditions and results. Use the aircraft-specific reference to explain why performance differs.','The student distinguishes the changed condition from other variables and supports a conclusion using the results.','Write a comparison of two conditions and the expected performance change.'),
('Weight and balance','Explain loading limits and calculate a supervised example with consistent units.','Aircraft-specific loading worksheet, calculator','Identify empty weight, load, moment, and center of gravity in the supplied example. Calculate two loading arrangements and compare each with the provided envelope.','The student shows the calculation steps, preserves units, and identifies whether the example lies within the supplied limits.','Explain why two equal-weight loads can produce different balance results.'),
('Using aviation regulations','Find and interpret the current rule relevant to a training scenario.','Current FAA references and instructor-selected scenarios','Distinguish regulations, advisory guidance, and aircraft limitations. Locate the current reference for two age-appropriate scenarios, explain the wording, and identify questions for a qualified instructor.','The student cites the relevant reference and distinguishes an academy skill award from an aviation certificate or authorization.','Record the reference and a plain-language explanation of one scenario.'),
('Build a complete flight plan','Combine route, weather, loading, performance, and communications into a simulated flight plan.','Chart, weather examples, loading worksheet, simulator','Prepare a short supervised flight plan. Brief the route, alternatives, weather, loading, and communications. Fly a simulated segment and debrief differences from the plan.','The student explains the plan, identifies missing information, and revises it when the instructor changes one condition.','Complete a portfolio entry with the plan and debrief.')]
cad=('Onshape foundations','Create and revise a dimensioned part with an editable feature history.','Onshape, paper, ruler','Sketch a dimensioned mounting plate, extrude it, add mounting holes, and use a named variable for one repeated dimension. Change the variable, inspect the rebuild, and export a test part in millimeters.','The student demonstrates an intentional dimension change, explains the Feature list, and verifies the export scale.','Save a version and annotate one model screenshot.')
battle=('Plan a combat robot','Compare robot archetypes and select a design against an agreed classroom brief.','Paper, dimensional limits, approved kit parts, Onshape','Compare a wedge, lifter, and other instructor-approved design using mass, traction, reach, and repairability. Sketch a layout, allocate component space, and document the selected concept. Powered mechanisms remain disabled during planning.','The student presents a dimensioned layout, a parts list, and a reasoned comparison against the class constraints.','Measure the supplied components and update the layout.')
def lesson(t):
 title,objective,materials,activity,check,homework=t
 plan=f'Objective\n{objective}\n\nPrepare\n{materials}. Check equipment, supervision requirements, and the previous lesson record before class.\n\n0–10 min · Brief and retrieve\nAsk students to explain the relevant prior skill. Agree on today’s objective and how success will be demonstrated.\n\n10–25 min · Explain and demonstrate\nIntroduce the essential vocabulary and demonstrate the first step. Ask students to predict the result before showing it.\n\n25–65 min · Guided practice\n{activity}\n\n65–80 min · Demonstrate and assess\n{check} Record individual evidence; participation alone does not award a skill.\n\n80–90 min · Debrief and reset\nCompare predictions with results. Record one next step, save the work, and restore the learning area.\n\nOptional homework\n{homework}\n\nInstructor-review draft · Adapt pacing to the students.\nSource: https://app.notion.com/p/Knowledge-Base-Courses-5fd9648b807a8345a9d80183a7c7460c'
 return dict(title=title,plan=plan,abilities=[])
plans={'uas-100':list(map(lesson,aero))+[lesson(cad)],'flt-100':list(map(lesson,flight)),'eng-101':[lesson(battle),lesson(cad)]}
Path('ios/BiotechSignal/Academy/Resources/academy-course-plans.json').write_text(json.dumps(plans,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
